From c92253d4b346b505fa9332bb52198027ce9a7dc1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Angelo Arifi <arifilabs@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 01:09:04 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] docs(FINDINGS): F-09 - the repack/GPU trade-off is a
 compute-placement choice, not a bug

Investigated the -77% prompt regression as a placement defect on the assumption that smarter
buffer selection would recover both the decode win and the prefill speed. It will not, and the
reason is worth writing down so nobody spends the same time twice.

The two buffer types are not better/worse versions of one thing: the host-visible buffer is CPU
memory the GPU can read, so batch matmuls offload to it; the repack buffer is an interleaved
layout only the CPU vector kernels understand. Picking one picks which processor does the batch
work. Prefill is a large batch and wants the GPU; decode is single-token, runs CPU-side either
way, and just wants the faster CPU layout. The right answer depends on the operation, which is
not known when the buffer is chosen at load.

Three real exits are recorded with their costs: keep both copies (works, doubles memory for the
affected tensors); enable repack only when no GPU backend exists at all (narrow but strictly
correct, and answerable at load time unlike per-tensor offload - the most likely next step);
or re-lay-out weights after prefill.

Origin: ArifiLabs lane-110D, code-optimisation pass
Measured-effect: analysis only, no code change. Evidence is the buffer accounting already in
OPTIONS-REGISTRY: repack ON gives Vulkan0 5.26 MiB / 286 graph splits, OFF gives 37.72 MiB /
335 - the batch matmuls moving off the GPU is visible in the split counts.
